# Status Reports — Monitoring and Controlling
#
# PMBOK Guide 6th ed., 4.5 Monitor and Control Project Work and 10.2 Manage
# Communications.

screen: status
title: Status Reports
phase: Monitoring and Controlling
process: 4.5 Monitor and Control Project Work
order: 90
requires: schedule
teaches: |
  How to report against a plan rather than describe activity, and what makes
  a status report worth someone's attention. By the end you should be able to
  explain why a report that is always green is not a report.
before: |
  A baseline to report against. Without one, a status report can only say
  what happened, never whether it was what was supposed to happen.
after: |
  The decisions your stakeholders make. A report exists to generate a
  decision, not to prove you were busy.
intro: |
  A status report compares where the project is against where the plan said it
  would be. Work performance data becomes information becomes a report: raw
  observations, then analysis, then something a stakeholder can act on.

  The useful ones are short, dated, and honest about the difference between
  planned and actual. A report that is always green is not a report.
source: |
  PMBOK Guide, 6th ed., process 4.5 Monitor and Control Project Work (work
  performance reports are an output) and 10.2 Manage Communications, which is
  how those reports reach stakeholders.

field reports
  prompt: Where is the project against the plan, this period?
  check column:rag: A report with no overall call is a description of activity. Green, amber or red is a judgement you have to make.
  label: Status reports
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: period
  tone_column: rag
  source: |
    Work performance reports (4.5 output): the physical or electronic
    representation of work performance information, compiled to raise
    awareness and generate decisions.
  why: |
    The RAG column is a judgement, not a calculation: green means the plan
    still holds, amber means it holds only if something changes, red means it
    does not hold. Stating it forces the call to be made rather than implied.

    Planned versus actual is the body of the report. The next-period column is
    what makes it a forecast instead of a history.
  if_blank: |
    Nobody can see a slip forming, and the first honest status report is the
    post-mortem.
  example: |
    Week of Sep 15 | Green | Charter approved, Initiating screens built | Planning screens | Qt deploy step
  column period
    label: Period
    type: line
    width: 2
  column reported_on
    label: Date
    type: date
    width: 2
  column rag
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Green=ok, Amber=warn, Red=danger
    width: 1
  column accomplished
    label: Completed this period
    type: text
    width: 5
  column planned_next
    label: Planned next period
    type: text
    width: 5
  column variance
    label: Variance from plan
    type: text
    width: 4
  column escalations
    label: Escalations / help needed
    type: text
    width: 4

field summary
  prompt: In one paragraph, how is it actually going?
  check words>=12: If this takes more than a paragraph, the project has a problem the reports are not naming.
  label: Current status summary
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    4.5 Monitor and Control Project Work: the current, stand-alone statement of
    where the project is.
  why: |
    One paragraph a stakeholder can read without opening anything else. If it
    takes more than a paragraph, the project has a problem the reports are not
    naming.
  if_blank: |
    Anyone asking "how is it going" gets a different answer depending on who
    they ask.
  example: |
    Initiating and Planning screens are usable on the tool's own record.
    Executing is in progress. Closeout date unchanged.
